"""Original security, observability, deployment, and validation teaching."""
from teaching.foundations import put, LESSONS
put(18, '''Threat modeling starts from assets, actors, trust boundaries, and abuse goals. For a multi-tenant document service, identify confidential files, credentials, processing workers, administrators, and storage capabilities. Ask how an attacker could read another tenant's file, forge a callback, exhaust extraction capacity, or alter an audit record. Prioritize by impact and plausible paths, then attach mitigations and tests. A diagram without a data inventory or actor privileges is an incomplete threat model. || Identify confidential document asset / Trace upload and extraction boundaries / Model cross-tenant abuse path / Attach control and verification evidence

A trust boundary changes the confidence or authority granted to input. Browser-to-server, server-to-storage, worker-to-model, and operator-to-production are distinct boundaries. Validate identity and relevant data each time a boundary is crossed; do not treat internal network origin as proof of legitimacy. Headers forwarded by a trusted proxy require a specific trust configuration. Uploaded files remain untrusted after storage and must be processed with appropriate resource and execution isolation. || Untrusted client input / Authenticated application boundary / Scoped storage and worker identity / Validate again at sensitive effect boundary

Authentication establishes identity; authorization decides permitted action on a particular resource in context. An organization administrator may manage their own tenant without any right to another tenant's files. Derive tenancy from authenticated membership and enforce it on every read, update, export, and background operation. Guess-resistant IDs reduce enumeration but do not supply authorization. Deny by default, keep policy decisions explicit, and test both allowed access and cross-tenant denial paths. || Validate caller identity / Resolve authorized tenant context / Query resource under that tenant / Authorize requested action before effect

Sessions commonly store server-side state referenced by a credential; tokens can carry claims verified under a protocol. Cookies are a browser transport/storage mechanism with security attributes, not an authentication model by themselves. Protect credentials with appropriate secure transport, cookie settings, CSRF defenses where relevant, and token validation. Logout and revocation behavior differ between stateful sessions and self-contained tokens. A signed token is not automatically current, audience-correct, or authorized for a resource. || Browser presents credential / Server validates issuer or session / Check expiry, audience and relevant policy / Resolve current authorization context

OAuth 2.0 concerns delegated authorization; OpenID Connect adds an identity layer. Do not use an access token as an identity assertion without the intended protocol and validation. For appropriate browser/native flows, use current best-practice guidance such as authorization code with PKCE, exact redirect handling, and library-based validation. Issuer, audience, signature, nonce or state requirements, and token type depend on the flow. Never invent a custom shortcut that confuses login identity with permission to a target API. || User authenticates with identity provider / Authorized code exchange follows protocol / Client validates intended identity artifact / API validates its own access token

RBAC grants permissions through roles; ABAC evaluates attributes such as tenant, resource classification, ownership, and context. Roles simplify administration but can become too broad; attribute policies can express finer rules while becoming harder to explain and test. A tenant-admin role must be bound to the tenant, not treated as global administration. Keep policy inputs trustworthy and test revocation and background jobs. A policy engine centralizes evaluation only if enforcement points actually call it correctly. || Actor role and tenant / Resource owner and classification / Policy evaluates action context / Enforcement point denies or permits effect

Least privilege limits identities to the resources and operations required for their work. An extraction worker may read one input object and write its output without listing every tenant's bucket. Separate deployment, runtime, and human administrative identities where useful. Short-lived scoped credentials reduce exposure, but also require reliable refresh and failure handling. Review effective permissions, inherited roles, and emergency access rather than only the intended configuration in a design document. || Identify required operation / Issue narrowly scoped permission / Perform auditable effect / Expire or revoke unused authority

Defense in depth places complementary controls around a risk: tenant-scoped queries, database permissions, signed object access, worker isolation, and audit evidence can all reduce cross-tenant harm. Repeating the same ineffective check at three layers is not three independent defenses. Avoid controls whose failures share one mistaken assumption, such as trusting client tenant_id everywhere. Understand fallback behavior so a failed security dependency does not silently bypass the protections it was meant to enforce. || Application checks membership / Storage enforces scoped access / Worker receives limited capability / Audit detects unexpected access patterns

Secrets need an owner, distribution mechanism, access policy, rotation, and revocation procedure. Keep credentials out of source, logs, command histories, and client bundles. Prefer managed identity or short-lived credentials when supported, while planning bootstrap and outage behavior. Rotation may require overlapping valid versions and verified retirement. Encrypting a secret file does little if its decryption key is accessible to every process that can read the file; key and permission boundaries matter. || Secret stored under controlled identity / Runtime obtains scoped access / Rotate with bounded overlap / Revoke and verify old credential rejection

Encryption in transit protects communication under a protocol; encryption at rest protects stored bytes under key and access assumptions. Neither prevents an authorized-but-compromised application from reading data it is allowed to decrypt. Identify termination points, key custody, backups, logs, and derived copies. A storage provider's at-rest encryption does not automatically encrypt an exported CSV sent elsewhere. Match controls to the threat model and use reviewed protocols and libraries instead of custom cryptography. || Protect transport hop / Protect persisted bytes and backups / Define key access boundary / Authorization still controls plaintext use

Passwords require purpose-built password hashing with salt and an appropriate work factor, not fast general-purpose hashing or reversible encryption. Use a maintained implementation and current OWASP guidance, choosing parameters that balance offline-guessing resistance with service capacity. Store the encoded algorithm and parameters for migration, and rehash when policy changes. Rate-limit verification and protect recovery flows. Do not invent fixed universal parameters in a textbook; hardware, libraries, and current guidance must be checked during deployment. || Password enters trusted verifier / Purpose-built salted password hash / Store algorithm and parameters / Verify and migrate under bounded resource policy

Key management includes generation, storage, access, rotation, versioning, backup, and destruction. Envelope encryption can separate data-encryption keys from a key-encryption authority, but the design must retain the right key versions to read permitted historical data. Deleting a key before verifying retention and recovery needs can make backups unusable. Conversely, retaining every key indefinitely may defeat deletion goals. Record which data and services depend on each key version and test rotation and restore paths. || Data encrypted under versioned key / Key protected by managed authority / Rotation preserves needed old versions / Retirement follows verified lifecycle

Network segmentation limits reachable paths and reduces some lateral movement. A worker network may reach storage and a model service but not the administrative database interface. Egress policy matters for uploaded-file processing and remote URL fetching because server-side requests can target internal services. Network controls complement application identity and authorization; an allowed connection should not grant every operation. Test actual reachability and DNS behavior, not only firewall rule descriptions. || Worker identity and subnet / Allow required storage route / Deny unrelated administrative path / Application still authorizes each operation

Zero-trust principles reject implicit authorization based solely on network location. Evaluate identity, resource, action, and relevant context at explicit boundaries, with limited privileges and observable policy decisions. The term is not a single product or a requirement to synchronously contact a central server for every packet. Cached credentials and policy need expiry and revocation decisions. Make trust assumptions inspectable rather than claiming that a service mesh or VPN automatically supplies the whole security architecture. || Network location is insufficient / Authenticate actor and workload / Authorize resource action / Re-evaluate under defined policy lifetime

API risks include broken object authorization, excessive data exposure, unsafe resource consumption, injection, and server-side request abuse. Validate inputs but also constrain how they are used. Parameterized SQL protects a query boundary; it does not authorize a tenant's access to the selected row. Bound expensive exports and validate callback destinations. Maintain negative tests for object IDs, property changes, quotas, and privileged endpoints so a syntactically valid request cannot bypass business security. || Valid JSON request / Attempt to access another tenant ID / Resource ownership check denies / Input syntax alone would have allowed it

Tenant isolation must survive every path: API lookup, cache key, job queue, object URL, search filter, export, telemetry, and administrative action. Carry trusted tenant context and revalidate resource relationships rather than accepting arbitrary tenant IDs in messages. Composite keys or database policies can add enforcement when correctly configured. A global cache keyed only by document ID can bypass an otherwise correct database query. Test two tenants with overlapping local IDs and adversarial cross-access attempts. || Tenant A document 7 / Tenant B document 7 / Cache and query include trusted tenant / Background job cannot change tenant scope

Data minimization retains only information required for a defined purpose. An observability event often needs an operation ID and error class, not a raw document or access token. Reducing retained sensitive data lowers exposure and makes deletion and recovery simpler. Avoid collecting fields merely because future analysis might find them useful. Document purpose, access, retention, and derived uses; privacy is affected by architecture choices long before an organization writes a retention policy. || Define business purpose / Select minimal necessary fields / Restrict derived telemetry / Delete when purpose ends

Audit trails record security-relevant actions with actor, scope, action, target, outcome, and relevant time or sequence. Protect integrity and access; do not let ordinary operators silently rewrite the evidence of their own privileged actions. Audit logs can themselves expose sensitive data, so store identifiers and necessary context rather than entire payloads. Logging “request received” is not evidence that an action committed. Record the actual outcome boundary and reconcile interrupted privileged workflows. || Actor attempts privileged action / Authorization decision recorded / Effect outcome linked to operation / Integrity and retention protect evidence

Privacy lifecycle includes collection, access, correction, retention, and deletion across raw and derived copies. A delete API returning success before all required deletion work is durable creates an ambiguous promise. Model deletion as a tracked workflow when it spans stores, backups, indexes, and caches. Legal obligations vary and need appropriate organizational review; this design discussion does not invent jurisdiction-specific rules. Test that restoring historical data does not silently resurrect deleted content without applying the deletion history. || Durable deletion intent / Remove authoritative and derived data / Track required retention exceptions / Restore reapplies current deletion decisions

Supply-chain security covers dependencies, build tools, artifacts, deployment permissions, and update paths. Pin or otherwise control dependency resolution, verify artifact identity, and keep build and runtime privileges appropriately narrow. A signed artifact proves a signing relationship, not that its code is harmless. Review provenance, vulnerability response, and the ability to revoke a compromised release. Avoid letting an untrusted pull request access deployment secrets merely because the normal branch build is trusted. || Source and dependencies selected / Controlled build produces artifact digest / Release verifies artifact identity / Runtime receives least required privilege

Security and usability interact through real user behavior. A protection that makes legitimate recovery impossible may drive unsafe workarounds; a convenience shortcut that treats every administrator as global creates cross-tenant risk. Evaluate the concrete journey, threat, and fallback. Progressive friction, scoped administration, clear recovery, and safe defaults can improve both. Do not reduce the trade-off to a score that permits violating a mandatory isolation rule; some constraints must remain hard requirements. || Identify user journey / Identify abuse opportunity / Design bounded safe recovery / Test both denial and legitimate completion

A secure-by-design review follows one sensitive operation end to end. For an export, verify actor and tenant, resource filters, object permissions, queue context, signed download lifetime, audit outcome, and deletion. Include failures such as revoked membership after enqueue and a worker retry after cancellation. Record assumptions and ownership of controls. A checklist helps coverage, but its evidence should be concrete tests and reviewed configurations rather than a declaration that the system is secure. || Export authorized and enqueued / Membership later revoked / Worker rechecks required policy / Download capability remains appropriately scoped''')

put(19, '''Monitoring tracks known signals and conditions; observability supports explaining system behavior from outputs and instrumentation. The distinction is a useful emphasis, not a universal product boundary. A dashboard that says CPU is normal may not explain a five-second payment request. Start from a user-visible symptom and connect operations, waits, dependencies, and state transitions. Instrumentation is valuable when it helps answer questions and act, not simply because more data is being collected. || User sees slow operation / Inspect service-level symptom / Follow dependency and queue evidence / Form and test a causal hypothesis

Logs record discrete events, metrics aggregate measurements, and traces relate operations along a path. They complement one another: a latency histogram reveals the population tail, a trace localizes one slow request, and structured logs explain a particular error. None alone guarantees a complete account. Sampling, dropped telemetry, inconsistent clocks, and privacy limits affect the evidence. Use stable operation and resource identities to connect signals without making unbounded identifiers metric labels. || Metric shows p99 increase / Trace exposes long database wait / Log links blocked operation / Combined evidence guides investigation

Structured logging uses named fields with stable meanings rather than relying only on free-form messages. Include event type, operation ID, tenant-safe scope, outcome, and relevant duration. Do not log credentials or raw sensitive documents as a debugging shortcut. A request-start log and request-finish log describe different boundaries; missing finish events can indicate interruption but not a specific business result. Bound volume and field sizes so a failure does not create a logging-induced outage. || Event type: payment_attempt / Operation ID and safe context / Explicit outcome and duration / Redact secrets and bound payload size

A distributed trace links spans representing work and waits across components. Parent-child relationships describe instrumentation structure; asynchronous work may need links rather than a misleading synchronous parent lifetime. A five-second root span with 4.6 seconds in pool acquisition suggests a different intervention from 4.6 seconds of database execution. Instrument queue wait and connection wait explicitly. A trace is one sampled execution, so compare distributions and errors before generalizing its cause to every slow request. || Root request: 5000 ms / Pool acquisition: 4600 ms / Query execution: 100 ms / Queueing dominates this sampled path

Trace IDs identify a trace, span IDs identify individual spans, and propagation carries the relevant context across boundaries. Validate or regenerate untrusted incoming context according to policy and avoid treating trace identity as authorization. For queues, propagate correlation or links so a later worker can be connected to the originating operation without implying it ran during the HTTP request. Standard propagation formats improve interoperability; they do not ensure every library or message transport is instrumented automatically. || Incoming request context / Server creates child span / Message carries trace relationship / Worker links later processing span

Golden signals commonly include latency, traffic, errors, and saturation. They describe user serving and resource pressure together. A service can have low error rate while latency grows because work queues rather than fails immediately. Break down outcomes and routes where needed so one high-volume cheap endpoint does not hide a critical low-volume failure. Definitions must identify eligible requests and useful results; counting every HTTP 200 as success can conceal a wrong or empty business response. || Traffic offered and completed / Latency distribution / Meaningful error outcomes / Saturation and queue evidence

RED focuses on rate, errors, and duration for request-serving components; USE focuses on utilization, saturation, and errors for resources. Apply the lens to the question. A database connection pool can be saturated while host CPU is low, explaining why increasing CPU does not help. Averages hide bursts, so examine time resolution and per-owner skew. These methods organize investigation rather than replace workload-specific indicators such as oldest unprocessed payment age. || RED describes request behavior / USE describes resource pressure / Pool saturated with low CPU / Join the two views on one workload

An SLI measures a chosen service behavior; an SLO sets a target; an error budget quantifies the allowed shortfall over a window. Define both good events and eligible events, including relevant failures. For a 99.9% target, a 1% bad-event rate consumes budget ten times as fast as the allowed 0.1% rate. Segment critical operations appropriately and ensure low-volume denominators are handled thoughtfully. An SLO is not a guarantee about every individual request. || Target bad fraction: 0.001 / Observed bad fraction: 0.01 / Burn rate: 10 / Sustained burn shortens remaining budget

Alerts should identify actionable user harm or imminent loss of an objective with a clear owner and response. Paging on every isolated CPU spike creates noise; paging on sustained fast error-budget burn can align response with impact. Use appropriate windows and thresholds, and pair urgent alerts with runbooks. Avoid alerts that fire constantly during known degradation without changing the action. Evaluate false positives, missed incidents, and the operational load imposed on responders. || Meaningful symptom crosses policy / Alert identifies owner and impact / Runbook gives first useful action / Feedback improves signal quality

Sampling and retention control telemetry cost but affect which questions can be answered. Head sampling decides early; tail sampling can select traces based on later observed behavior but needs buffering and capacity. High-cardinality labels such as unique request IDs can create unbounded time-series growth. Keep detailed identities in suitable logs or traces and use bounded dimensions for metrics. Protect telemetry ingestion during outages so error bursts do not consume all application or observability capacity. || Choose bounded metric dimensions / Sample detailed traces under policy / Retain necessary investigation evidence / Limit ingestion cost and failure amplification

A dashboard should answer a decision question: is the service meeting its objective, what changed, and where is capacity constrained? Put user outcomes before infrastructure vanity metrics. Show denominators, time windows, units, data gaps, and deployment markers. A chart without a stable definition can mislead during an incident. Include drill-down paths and ownership, but avoid hundreds of panels that obscure the one signal requiring action. Verify dashboards against known synthetic or historical scenarios. || Service objective and outcomes / Workload and deployment context / Resource and dependency drill-down / Clear units and missing-data indication

Profiling samples or instruments CPU, allocation, blocking, and other runtime behavior. A profile of one representative workload can reveal expensive parsing or lock contention hidden by request metrics. Sampling overhead and production privacy need consideration. A function using most CPU in a profile is not necessarily on the latency-critical path; I/O waiting may dominate response time. Compare profile findings with traces and completion rate, and re-measure after a focused optimization. || Representative workload / CPU or allocation profile / Candidate expensive mechanism / Validate effect on useful latency and throughput

Diagnose a slow application by splitting waiting from execution. Inspect queue time, pool acquisition, lock wait, query plan, rows processed, network, and serialization. If p99 rises from 200 ms to five seconds while CPU remains modest, a saturated pool or lock can be plausible but requires evidence. Compare slow and normal paths and correlate with changes. Do not restart every component before preserving useful evidence and understanding whether the action could worsen an overload. || Observe 5 s p99 / Separate wait and execution spans / Inspect plan and lock evidence / Test the specific bottleneck hypothesis

Incident response coordinates mitigation, communication, evidence, and decision ownership. Assign an incident lead for sufficiently complex events, maintain a timeline, and reduce harmful changes. Restore useful service with a justified reversible intervention while preserving data correctness. A rollback is not safe if it misreads a migrated schema. Track uncertain business outcomes such as pending charges for reconciliation after serving recovers; ending an infrastructure alert does not mean the complete incident outcome is resolved. || Declare impact and ownership / Stabilize and mitigate / Preserve timeline and evidence / Reconcile data before closing incident

On-call needs actionable alerts, access, runbooks, escalation, and sustainable ownership. A runbook should specify diagnosis, safe actions, stop conditions, and verification, not merely “restart the service.” Rehearse recovery paths and ensure emergency access is audited. Assign service owners who can improve recurring failure mechanisms. Operational burden is architecture evidence: repeated manual reconciliation or night-time cache rebuilds may justify changing the design rather than making the instructions longer. || Alert reaches accountable responder / Runbook identifies safe first steps / Escalation covers missing expertise / Repeated toil informs design changes

Root-cause investigation tests explanations against evidence and distinguishes triggers from contributing conditions. A deployment may trigger a failure because an unbounded queue or incompatible migration made the system fragile. Correlation is a starting hypothesis, not proof. Ask what changed, which requests failed, what resource path explains the effect, and whether the proposed fix changes that mechanism. Several interacting causes are common; a single short label can hide the conditions needed to prevent recurrence. || Timeline identifies trigger / Trace and state explain mechanism / Alternative hypotheses tested / Corrective work targets contributing conditions

A blameless postmortem records impact, timeline, detection, mitigation, and contributing system conditions without pretending decisions were made with hindsight. Include what helped as well as what failed, then assign specific changes and evidence. “Be more careful” is not an architectural mitigation. A postmortem should explain why safeguards did not prevent or limit harm and how the system will behave differently next time. Protect sensitive customer and employee information in the resulting record. || Describe actual impact / Reconstruct available information / Identify missing or weak safeguards / Verify corrective actions against failure scenario

Capacity monitoring observes rates, resource pressure, queue age, growth, and surviving capacity during failure. A low fleet CPU average can hide one saturated shard or a full connection pool. Alert on meaningful approach to a limit with enough lead time for the available response. Track storage growth and compaction or backup overhead as well as foreground traffic. Forecasts should retain uncertainty and be updated when access patterns, release behavior, or business events change. || Measure per-owner pressure / Track growth and queue age / Compare scaling or procurement lead time / Reserve capacity for failure and maintenance

Asynchronous observability follows a business operation beyond the original request. Link submission, queue delivery, attempts, stage commits, retries, and terminal outcomes using stable identities. Separate queue wait from processing duration and record duplicate or stale-attempt rejection. A trace ending at HTTP 202 says little about whether an extraction or refund eventually finished. Define end-to-end age and completion indicators, and maintain a way to locate stuck operations across brokers, workers, databases, and external providers. || Submission returns operation ID / Message links worker attempt / Stage outcome records same identity / End-to-end completion age reveals stuck work''')

put(20, '''Development, staging, and production serve different purposes but need deliberate compatibility. Staging should exercise relevant protocols, migrations, identity, and failure paths without using uncontrolled production secrets or personal data. Exact scale parity may be impractical, so document which behaviors it cannot validate. Configuration differences are a common source of surprises; keep environment-specific values separate from application semantics. A passing local test does not establish production connection limits, disk behavior, or network policy. || Local behavior test / Staging integration and release rehearsal / Production controlled rollout / Differences are documented and measured

A virtual machine presents virtualized hardware with its guest operating system; containers commonly isolate processes while sharing a host kernel. Both need image lifecycle, resource controls, identity, network, and storage design. A container is not a tiny VM with all the same isolation guarantees. Choose based on workload, operating expertise, startup and isolation needs. Multiple containers on one host share failure and resource domains, while VM boundaries still do not remove shared infrastructure risks. || Host hardware or hypervisor / VM includes guest OS / Containers share host kernel / Choose required isolation and ownership

A container packages an executable environment while runtime controls constrain CPU, memory, filesystem, and network access. Keep persistent state outside an ephemeral writable layer when it must survive replacement. Image reproducibility and configuration matter more than assuming packaging alone makes deployments deterministic. Resource limits can terminate or throttle work, so applications need graceful shutdown and durable progress. Running as a privileged user or mounting broad host access can weaken the intended boundary. || Immutable image selected / Runtime applies identity and limits / Ephemeral process executes / Durable state survives elsewhere

Kubernetes reconciles declared objects with cluster state through its control plane and node agents. Pods, services, controllers, and scheduling provide useful primitives, but they do not automatically supply correct application transactions, backups, tenant isolation, or SLOs. Understand the platform responsibilities you operate or delegate. Start with simpler deployment machinery when it already satisfies the workload; adopting an orchestrator creates a control plane, configuration surface, and operational dependency that must be justified. || Declare desired workload / Control plane records desired state / Nodes run scheduled pods / Application still owns business correctness

The control plane decides configuration and placement; the data plane serves application traffic. A control-plane outage can stop new scheduling while already running workloads continue under some conditions. Avoid unnecessary synchronous control-plane dependencies in every request. Cached configuration needs an explicit validity and update policy, especially for security-sensitive changes. Draw the two paths separately so a claimed serving redundancy does not conceal a single configuration or credential dependency needed for recovery. || Control plane changes desired state / Data plane serves current configuration / Control outage freezes some changes / Recovery still needs valid state and identity

A scheduler assigns work to eligible resources according to constraints; service discovery exposes reachable service endpoints. Placement decisions should include capacity, affinity, anti-affinity, and failure domains where required. A scheduled process may not yet be ready to receive traffic. Discovery can lag termination or startup, so clients and proxies still need bounded failure handling. Do not infer state consistency from successful scheduling: two replicas can run correctly while pointing at independent local databases. || Scheduler selects eligible node / Process starts and initializes / Readiness permits discovery endpoint / Client handles stale endpoint information

Autoscaling can change instances, worker concurrency, or provisioned capacity. Select signals related to the workload, preserve safe minimums, and include warmup and draining. Scaling down must stop admission, respect deadlines, and transfer durable work rather than killing unrecorded jobs. Database and provider limits can cap useful growth even if platform capacity is available. Protect total downstream concurrency and spend; an autoscaler responding to retries can otherwise amplify an incident. || Observe meaningful demand / Add bounded ready capacity / Protect downstream limits / Drain and persist before scale-down

Configuration and secrets need versioned, observable delivery with validation. A bad configuration can break every healthy replica without a code release. Validate new values, roll out gradually where appropriate, and retain a recovery path. Secrets require stronger access and logging discipline than ordinary configuration. Reload semantics matter: some values can change in place, while others require a new process or connection. Record the effective configuration version on telemetry so incidents can be correlated to actual behavior. || Validate configuration version / Deliver under scoped identity / Roll out and observe effects / Revert or restart according to contract

Immutable infrastructure replaces instances from defined artifacts rather than relying on undocumented manual changes. It improves repeatability when artifacts, configuration, and provisioning are controlled. It does not mean runtime data is immutable or that rollback is universally safe. Keep persistent state and migration strategy explicit. Emergency changes should be captured into the source of configuration or they disappear at replacement and create drift. Verify what an instance actually runs, not only what deployment intended. || Build identified artifact / Provision replacement instance / Apply versioned configuration / Retire old instance after verification

Infrastructure as Code describes desired resources and policy in reviewable form. Plans help expose changes, but provider behavior, state management, privileges, and drift still matter. A harmless-looking resource replacement can delete a stateful service unless lifecycle and backups are understood. Separate secrets from ordinary tracked configuration, lock or coordinate concurrent updates, and verify applied state. The code is one input to operations; recovery also needs access to its state and credentials when infrastructure is damaged. || Review declared resource change / Inspect replacement and permission effects / Apply under controlled ownership / Verify actual resources and drift

CI checks changes; delivery prepares releasable artifacts; deployment moves an artifact into an environment. A pipeline should preserve identity from source through built artifact to running version, run meaningful tests, and apply scoped permissions. An integration test passing in one environment does not prove a migration is safe during mixed-version traffic. Include the checks that address real release risks and avoid untrusted build inputs gaining release credentials. Repeatable automation reduces manual variation, but its own failures need a recovery procedure. || Source revision / Verified build artifact digest / Controlled environment promotion / Observe effective running release

Rolling deployment replaces instances gradually; blue-green prepares a parallel environment and switches traffic; canary exposes a controlled subset before wider rollout. All need compatible schemas, traffic drain, capacity, and verification. A canary that receives only cheap requests can miss a critical-path regression. Define cohorts and stop conditions using meaningful indicators. Blue-green duplicates runtime capacity but does not automatically duplicate or safely roll back shared mutable data. Select by failure risk and operating cost. || Prepare candidate version / Route representative bounded traffic / Compare outcomes and invariants / Expand, pause or roll back under policy

Feature flags separate some release and exposure decisions. They need ownership, safe defaults, scope, observability, and removal policy. A flag does not replace authorization: letting the browser enable a privileged server operation is unsafe. Combinations create a testing surface, and stale flags accumulate complexity. When disabling a feature after it has written new state, ensure old code understands that state or provide a migration path. Runtime configuration is itself part of the release risk. || Code deployed with flag off / Enable for scoped cohort / Observe behavior and stored state / Retire flag after safe stabilization

Rollback restores code or configuration only when state and contracts remain compatible. Use expand-and-contract database changes: add compatible structures, deploy code that can operate across the transition, backfill and verify, then remove obsolete fields after old readers are gone. Destructive schema changes can make old code unusable. Long backfills need rate control and resumable progress. A rollback button should identify the affected data and protocol assumptions rather than imply time can be reversed for every side effect. || Expand compatible schema / Deploy mixed-version-safe code / Backfill and validate / Contract only after old readers retire

Environment configuration should change endpoints, credentials, capacity, and declared policy without silently changing business semantics. Validate types and required fields at startup or controlled reload. Avoid a pile of environment-specific conditionals that no test environment reproduces. Use explicit configuration versions and safe defaults, especially for security and data retention. A missing value should fail clearly when it would otherwise route data to the wrong tenant, region, or storage account. || One application behavior / Environment supplies scoped settings / Validation rejects unsafe omission / Telemetry records effective configuration

The Twelve-Factor App is methodology for service-oriented application operation: explicit dependencies and configuration, backing services, stateless processes where appropriate, logs as event streams, and related deployment practices. It is guidance rather than a universal law for every application. Stateful workloads and modern platforms require additional decisions about persistence, identity, resource limits, and recovery. Apply the intent to improve repeatability and ownership; do not claim that meeting twelve labels proves reliability or security. || Explicit dependencies and config / Replaceable application process / Durable backing service ownership / Operational guidance still needs validation

Serverless compute delegates some provisioning and execution management to a platform while preserving limits and failure semantics. Functions can be retried or interrupted, and scaling may create too many concurrent calls to a database. Externalize durable state, bound downstream concurrency, and use stable effect identity. Compare sustained cost, startup latency, portability, and operational responsibility with container or VM options. The serverless label does not imply zero servers, infinite capacity, or freedom from observability and security design. || Managed trigger invokes code / Execution has limits and retries / Durable outcome stored externally / Concurrency respects downstream capacity

Cloud and on-premises choices distribute ownership differently. Compare procurement, data placement, network, managed capabilities, staff expertise, compliance needs, recovery, and exit cost under the same workload. Cloud elasticity is useful only within provisioning and quota constraints; on-premises capacity can be efficient for some predictable needs but requires equipment lifecycle and disaster planning. Avoid generic price claims without current rates and assumptions. This atlas models illustrative unit costs rather than claiming a provider is universally cheapest. || Define workload and constraints / Assign operational responsibilities / Compare lifecycle cost and recovery / Include growth and exit assumptions

A managed service transfers selected tasks, not business responsibility. The provider may operate patches or replication while the application still chooses acknowledgment settings, schema, access, retention, backup validation, and regional recovery. Read the actual service contract and failure boundaries. Self-management gives control while requiring expertise and on-call work. Compare what remains your responsibility in either choice, and test client behavior under maintenance, throttling, failover, and credential changes. || Provider operates specified layers / Application configures required semantics / Team owns data and access decisions / Recovery evidence spans both responsibilities

Deployment topology places processes, state, trust boundaries, routes, and failure domains on actual infrastructure. A logical module diagram cannot show whether two replicas share a host, zone, or database. Include ingress, egress, TLS termination, private paths, and operational access. A topology should explain what happens when one placement disappears and how replacement obtains state and configuration. Keep control and data flow meanings clear; every arrow should identify the protocol or dependency it represents. || Client ingress path / Application placement and identity / Data storage failure domain / Recovery and administration routes

Platform engineering provides reusable paths for teams to build and operate services safely. Useful capabilities include artifact delivery, identity, telemetry, resource policy, and tested deployment patterns. Treat internal developers as users and provide escape hatches with clear ownership rather than an opaque mandatory framework for every workload. Platform abstractions must expose relevant limits and failure behavior. Measure adoption, reliability, and operating burden; another layer is valuable only when it simplifies work without hiding essential decisions. || Service team needs common capability / Platform offers documented contract / Limits and ownership stay visible / Feedback improves the reusable path''')

put(21, '''A performance budget allocates acceptable latency, resource, and cost across an operation under a named workload. An assumed 200 ms budget might reserve 30 ms for edge and network, 20 ms for queues, 100 ms for data access, and 50 ms for computation and response. These are design assumptions to validate, not measured benchmark facts. Include tails and failure cases. A budget that ignores pool wait or serialization can be exhausted before the supposed main operation begins. || End-to-end target: 200 ms / Edge and network: 30 ms / Queue and data: 120 ms / Compute and response: 50 ms

CPU, memory, disk, network, and databases can each limit useful work. High CPU can indicate computation or contention; low CPU can coexist with long lock or I/O waits. Memory pressure can trigger allocation stalls or termination. Follow the critical path and compare throughput with resource and queue evidence. A bottleneck tree is a set of hypotheses to test, not a justification for changing every component. Fixing one resource often exposes the next limit. || Symptom: rising latency / CPU executing or waiting? / Inspect memory, I/O and locks / Verify limiting stage with focused change

Profiling identifies where runtime work occurs; benchmarking compares behavior under a controlled workload. Define hardware or capacity, data distribution, warmup, concurrency, duration, success criteria, and versions. A microbenchmark of one function does not establish application throughput. Include variability and resource cost, and compare like-for-like service promises. If a result changes after a dependency or schema change, preserve enough setup information to reproduce the explanation rather than presenting a single impressive number. || Specify representative workload / Control versions and resources / Collect distributions and outcomes / Reproduce before generalizing result

Load tests exercise expected demand; stress tests explore limits; spike tests expose response to abrupt change; soak tests reveal long-duration issues such as leaks or compaction. Each answers a different question. Use a load generator that does not silently reduce offered load when the system slows unless that closed-loop behavior matches real clients. Check generator capacity and include errors, abandoned requests, and queue age. A high request count with wrong business results is not a successful performance test. || Expected steady load / Beyond-capacity stress / Abrupt spike / Long soak exposes accumulated resource problems

Benchmark pitfalls include unrealistic data, warmed caches without disclosure, coordinated omission, tiny samples, missing failures, and incompatible versions. Coordinated omission can occur when the generator waits on a slow response before issuing work that should have arrived independently, understating delay. Report distributions and methodology, not fabricated precision. Change one factor or use a justified experimental design. A result is evidence for the tested conditions and does not automatically extrapolate to a new traffic mix or failure state. || Workload arrival model declared / Slow requests remain counted / Errors and tails included / Report conditions and uncertainty

Tail analysis examines distributions and critical dependencies rather than only means. Distinguish a rare expensive route from a broad slowdown, and separate queue wait, service time, network, and fan-out. Merging percentile summaries incorrectly can hide fleet tails. Percentile estimates require enough samples for the question being asked. A p99 regression from 200 ms to five seconds should be investigated with population metrics and representative traces, not explained from one anecdotal slow request. || Compare population distributions / Segment affected operations / Inspect wait and execution traces / Test the proposed mechanism

Throughput approaches a ceiling when a limiting resource cannot complete work faster. Additional offered traffic then becomes rejection or queue growth. A queue is not extra service capacity. Find the goodput knee by observing successful outcomes, latency, and saturation under increasing demand, then reserve margin for variability and failure. A measured maximum in a short benchmark is not a safe production target. Include maintenance, background work, and the surviving capacity of the promised failure scenario. || Offered traffic rises / Goodput approaches resource ceiling / Queues and latency rise / Operate with justified headroom and admission

Tuning improves an implementation within its architecture; an architectural change alters ownership, placement, or major interaction. An index, batch size, or shorter transaction may resolve a bottleneck with less migration risk than sharding. Conversely, an unavoidably centralized invariant can require a different business or partitioning model. Measure before and after, and count correctness, operations, and migration cost alongside speed. The most sophisticated topology is not automatically the most defensible intervention. || Diagnose expensive mechanism / Try justified local improvement / Re-measure remaining limit / Change ownership only when evidence requires it

Horizontal scaling adds coordination, baseline replicas, discovery, and often network traffic. Its cost curve depends on utilization and shared bottlenecks. Compare a larger single deployment, a replicated stateless tier with one data owner, and a partitioned architecture under identical workload and objectives. The last may cost more at small scale while extending the eventual ceiling. Use explicit illustrative prices if actual quotes are unavailable, and keep failure headroom and operational labor assumptions visible. || Same useful workload / Single larger node option / Replicated application option / Partitioned option adds coordination cost

Total cost includes compute, storage, replicas, indexes, backups, networking, requests, telemetry, and operational work. A large response sent frequently can dominate egress despite cheap computation. Trace the cost path just as you trace latency. A cache may reduce origin compute while increasing memory and invalidation work. Do not quote live provider pricing without current verification; this atlas's worked prices are hypothetical arithmetic inputs and should be replaced with real quotes for a procurement decision. || Compute and persistence cost / Transfer and request cost / Telemetry and backup cost / Staffing and recovery obligations

Cost per request can obscure the business outcome if retries or failures inflate the denominator. Prefer cost per successful order, delivered document, or another meaningful unit, while still analyzing technical costs. With an assumed monthly cost of 3000 units and 150000 successful outcomes, unit cost is 0.02. If half the attempts fail, dividing by all attempts understates the cost of useful work. Define period, allocation, and included operating expenses. || Monthly cost: 3000 units / Successful outcomes: 150000 / Cost per outcome: 0.02 units / Attempts are a different denominator

Reserved capacity can lower some unit rates in exchange for commitment and utilization risk; elastic capacity handles variable demand with provisioning delay and possible premiums. Compare baseline demand, peak duration, growth uncertainty, and failure capacity. A reservation sized for an occasional peak can sit idle, while purely reactive elasticity can miss a short burst. Mixed strategies often fit, but the correct balance requires measured traffic and current commercial terms rather than a universal recommendation. || Predictable baseline demand / Variable burst demand / Compare commitment and delay / Reserve only justified capacity and headroom

Cost-performance optimization seeks a feasible point under service, security, and maintainability constraints. A cheaper design that misses the latency objective or loses acknowledged payments is not equivalent. Vary cache size, batching, compression, placement, and instance shape with measurements. Beware local improvements that increase downstream cost, such as compressed writes requiring excessive decompression on every read. Record the workload and objective so future changes do not reuse an optimization outside the conditions that made it sensible. || Fix service constraints / Compare measured resource options / Count downstream and operating effects / Choose a feasible efficient point

Resource efficiency includes avoiding idle work, unnecessary transfer, redundant computation, and over-retention. Sustainability depends on more than a single energy or carbon estimate; hardware utilization, region, time, lifecycle, and data quality affect claims. Do not invent environmental measurements. Useful engineering steps include bounding retries, deleting unused data, right-sizing capacity, and batching appropriate background work without violating deadlines. Evaluate the complete effect so saving CPU does not create much larger network or storage demand. || Remove unnecessary work / Measure full resource path / Preserve service obligations / Report only supported efficiency evidence

Unit tests isolate local behavior; integration tests verify real collaborators; contract tests verify boundary expectations; end-to-end tests exercise complete journeys. A test pyramid is guidance about feedback cost and scope, not a mandated numerical ratio. Use a fake provider for business decline logic and real database tests for atomicity. A large suite that mirrors private method calls can impede refactoring while missing the lost-response history. Choose tests by the failure each would detect and the evidence it provides. || Local invariant test / Real storage integration / Boundary compatibility contract / End-to-end business journey

Fault injection tests behavior when a dependency is slow, unavailable, inconsistent, or interrupted. Place faults at meaningful boundaries: after database commit but before acknowledgment, during partition transfer, or after a worker lease expires. Killing a process only before work starts misses the dangerous windows. Define acceptable outcomes and stop conditions. Combine fault evidence with data reconciliation; an application that recovers request serving while silently losing jobs has not passed the full business contract. || Select failure window / Inject bounded interruption / Verify allowed state and responses / Reconcile all accepted business operations

Data validation compares source and destination identities, versions, aggregates, and domain invariants. Row counts alone cannot detect swapped ownership, stale updates, or equal-count omissions and duplicates. For a ledger, validate balanced postings and unique external operation identity; for a search index, compare source version and deletion state. Run checks after migration, replay, and recovery. Sampling can reduce cost but weakens the claim, so state whether evidence covers all records or only a sample. || Compare record identities / Compare source versions / Validate domain aggregate or invariant / Report scope and discrepancies

Architecture fitness functions continuously test required properties such as allowed imports, tenant-scoped queries, API compatibility, or recovery time. A useful function protects a reasoned architectural characteristic without freezing every incidental class relationship. Some properties need runtime drills or manual review rather than a static rule. Keep tests aligned with current decisions and update them when an ADR legitimately changes the architecture. Passing a collection of checks is evidence for their scope, not a universal architecture certification. || Architectural requirement named / Appropriate automated or exercised check / Change evaluated against requirement / Review outdated or overly rigid checks

Regression testing preserves important behavior as implementations change. Include error, concurrency, compatibility, and recovery histories that have caused harm, not only the original happy path. Use representative data and maintain test meaning during refactoring. A performance regression test needs stable enough conditions and tolerances to avoid noise-driven failures. When a requirement changes, update the expected behavior explicitly rather than deleting a failing test without explaining which contract is now different. || Known required history / Change applied / Same observable obligation checked / Legitimate requirement change is documented

Validate the design against its original requirements using a traceable evidence matrix. Every promise should identify a test, measurement, review, or unresolved assumption. Compare functional behavior, latency distributions, capacity, recovery, security, cost, and operational burden under the stated workload. A diagram review and a load test provide different evidence. If a target remains unmeasured, label it as an objective rather than reporting it as achieved. This closes the loop from design hypothesis to defensible engineering decision. || Requirement and assumption / Specific verification evidence / Observed result and limitation / Revise design or claim accordingly''')
